WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.790
HTTP clients often carry authentication material: API keys, bearer tokens, authorization headers, cookies, client certificates, refresh tokens,

2
00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.187
service credentials, or signed request metadata.

3
00:00:09.187 --> 00:00:17.575
These values connect the local application to remote authority, so they should be handled as secrets rather than ordinary configuration strings.

4
00:00:17.575 --> 00:00:21.569
Credentials should come from approved configuration and secret-management mechanisms.

5
00:00:21.569 --> 00:00:27.960
Hardcoded keys, debug defaults, copied sample tokens, and shared service credentials make review and rotation harder.

6
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:38.345
A secure design identifies where the credential is stored, who can read it, how it is deployed, how it is rotated, and which environment or service

7
00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:39.543
it belongs to.

8
00:00:39.543 --> 00:00:40.342
Scope matters.

9
00:00:40.342 --> 00:00:45.135
Tokens and service identities should grant only the operations the client needs.

10
00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:48.331
Development, staging, and production credentials should be separated.

11
00:00:48.331 --> 00:00:53.523
Credentials for unrelated services should not be reused just because it is convenient.

12
00:00:53.523 --> 00:01:01.512
Expiration and rotation behavior should be planned so the application handles renewal or replacement without exposing values or failing unpredictably.

13
00:01:01.512 --> 00:01:05.107
Cookies require the same care in non-browser clients.

14
00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:13.495
A client that stores or forwards cookies should have a clear policy for which domains, paths, lifetimes, and services are involved.

15
00:01:13.495 --> 00:01:19.886
Accidental credential forwarding can turn a simple redirect or shared client object into a security problem.

16
00:01:19.886 --> 00:01:22.682
Logging is a common place for mistakes.

17
00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:30.271
Authorization headers, cookies, bearer tokens, private keys, signed request material, and sensitive response data should not appear in logs,

18
00:01:30.271 --> 00:01:33.067
exceptions, crash reports, traces, or support bundles.

19
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:40.656
Useful diagnostics can record safe categories, endpoint labels, status classes, timing, and correlation IDs without exposing the credential itself.

20
00:01:40.656 --> 00:01:50.642
The goal is to make support possible while keeping secret values out of durable records that many people and systems may later access during normal

21
00:01:50.642 --> 00:01:51.840
production operations safely.
